Both probes separate only weakly. Held-out all-caps answers have a median score of 0.84, while the 99th percentile of no-comma answers is 1.05 and of "at least" capital-word answers 0.90. The capital-word "fewer than" probe has a similar overlap. Code stays low (max 0.43–0.60).

Before concluding the signal is weak, I suspect my labels. Pass 4's categories came from each prompt's primary instruction, so a no-comma text with a second all-caps instruction would be labelled no-comma even though its answer is in capitals, a negative that is really a positive. I'll recompute every text's instruction flags from the prompt metadata (pass 4 texts index pass-3 prompts) and count how many texts have mixed labels. If that's common, I'll refit with labels taken from the instruction flags.

```python
# Hypothesis: label noise — some pass-4 'NC'/'OTH' texts also carry capital/capword instructions, inflating negative scores.
def flags_from_types(tl):
    s = set((t[0], t[1] if len(t) > 1 else '') for t in tl)
    return dict(cap=any(t[0] == 'capital' for t in s), cwl=('capword', 'less than') in s, cwa=('capword', 'at least') in s,
                nc=any(t[0] == 'no_comma' for t in s), lc=any(t[0] == 'lowercase' for t in s))
fl_text = {}
for i, m in enumerate(meta4):
    if m['cat'] in ('code', 'math', 'mc'): fl_text[1000 + i] = dict(cap=False, cwl=False, cwa=False, nc=False, lc=False); continue
    pm = p3_meta[m['k']]; fl_text[1000 + i] = flags_from_types([(x['type'], x.get('rel', '')) for x in pm['insts']])
for i, m in enumerate(meta7):
    fl_text[i] = flags_from_types(m['types'])
mix = collections.Counter((LLc, tuple(k for k, v in fl_text[t].items() if v)) for t, LLc in LL.drop_duplicates('text')[['text', 'cat']].values)
for (c, f), n_ in sorted(mix.items()): print(c, f, n_)
